## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key finding is that while the National Testing Agency (NTA) did conduct the NEET 2020 and an error did occur involving a candidate initially declared failed who was later recognized as the topper in the Scheduled Tribe (ST) category, this candidate was **not** the overall all-India topper. The official all-India topper (rank 1) was Soyeb Aftab, who was never reported as failed. The NTA itself labeled broader claims of result errors as "fake, fabricated."

The information we have is sufficient to reach a conclusion. The claim conflates two distinct facts: an error involving an ST category topper and the identity of the overall all-India topper. The claim is technically true in that a "major blunder" occurred and a topper was initially declared failed, but it misleads by omitting the critical context that this was a category topper, not the overall national topper. Including this context creates a significantly different and more accurate impression.

Therefore, the claim is an example of cherry-picking. `cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The fact-check found that while an error did occur in the NEET 2020 results, it involved an ST category topper, [Mridul Rawat](https://www.livemint.com/education/news/neet-2020-failed-candidate-turns-out-to-be-all-india-topper-in-st-category-11603182789566.html), being initially declared failed—not the overall all-India topper. The official national topper was [Soyeb Aftab](https://www.businesstoday.in/latest/trends/story/major-blunder-in-neet-results-student-who-failed-found-to-be-the-topper-276333-2020-10-20), who was never reported as failed. The [NTA labeled broader claims of result errors as "fake, fabricated"](https://www.ndtv.com/education/claims-of-errors-in-neet-2020-results-fake-fabricated-nta-2313136). The claim omits this critical context, making it an example of cherry-picking.